---
// THE EDUCATION PAGE. Everything conceptual about a seed: how one is actually
// built, the four options for where its randomness comes from, what your device
// allows, why you would bother, and how it goes wrong. No procedures — those are the tool page, and a
// procedure written in two places is a procedure that will disagree with
// itself the first time a vendor moves a menu.
//
// EVERGREEN BY CONSTRUCTION. Nothing here names a specific incident. The
// advisory callout renders only while an advisory is active, so when one is
// retired this page does not quietly become a period piece.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import LessonNav from '../../components/LessonNav.astro';
import LessonEyebrow from '../../components/LessonEyebrow.astro';
import LessonCrumb from '../../components/LessonCrumb.astro';
import RuleBand from '../../components/RuleBand.astro';
import RuleRef from '../../components/RuleRef.astro';
import {
  diceVerified, methods, methodCount,
  ratedDevicesForMethod, devicesWithNoDicePath, devicesOutsideDiceQuestion,
  kit, failureModes, failureModeCount,
  seedLengthByKey, WORDLIST_SIZE, bitsPerWord,
} from '../../data/dice.js';
import { seedCosts } from '../../data/dice-table.js';
import { shortName } from '../../data/wallets.js';
import LessonClose from '../../components/LessonClose.astro';
import Typo from '../../components/demos/Typo.astro';
import Seed from '../../components/demos/Seed.astro';
import { activeAdvisories } from '../../data/advisories.js';
import { numberWord } from '../../data/numbers.js';

const advisory = activeAdvisories.find((a) => a.vendor === 'Coinkite') || null;

// Device support per option, joined from the same data /wallets filters on, so
// a tile can never disagree with the comparison table.
//
// The short-name rule is wallets.js's, not this page's. It used to be a local
// `trim` here, and check-device-coverage.py has to recognise these devices on
// the built page by the form the page prints.
const trim = shortName;
const optionTiles = methods.map((m) => ({
  ...m,
  devices: m.key === 'device' ? null : ratedDevicesForMethod(m.key).map((d) => trim(d.name)),
}));
const noPath = devicesWithNoDicePath.map((d) => trim(d.name));
// The THIRD state — devices the question does not apply to. Derived, because the
// two lists above are derived and a hand-typed third would be the one that rots.
// Bitkey fell out of both until 2026-08-01: eleven of the twelve devices we rate
// were enumerated on this page and the twelfth was simply absent.
const outside = devicesOutsideDiceQuestion.map((d) => ({ name: trim(d.name) }));
const longCost = seedCosts.find((c) => c.words === 24);

// LAYER 1, added 2026-08-03. This page ran options → why → what you need
// — layers 2 and 3 with the mechanism missing, so a reader could pick an option
// without ever being told what a seed IS. Every figure below is computed by
// mnemonicShape() rather than typed, including the count of valid final words,
// which is the one people assume is wrong.
const s24 = seedLengthByKey['24'];
const s12 = seedLengthByKey['12'];
---
<Base
  title="How a seed is made — and where its randomness comes from · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description="A seed is one very large random number, written down as words. How that actually works, the four ways to decide where the number comes from — from letting the device pick to choosing it all yourself — and what your hardware actually allows."
  verified={diceVerified}
>
  <LessonCrumb href="/learn/generate-your-seed" />
  <section class="prose">
    <LessonEyebrow />
    <h1>How a seed is made — and where its <span class="accent">randomness</span> comes from</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Your seed is one very large random number. Everything else — every address, every key, every coin you
      will ever hold — is worked out from it. Normally the device picks that number and you have no way to
      check that it picked well. You do not have to leave it at that.
    </p>
    {/* Inert today — this lesson owns no rule, and RuleBand renders nothing for
        it. Present so a future rule assignment behaves like every other page
        (the convention bitcoin-keys already follows). */}
    <RuleBand />
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="how">The three steps that turn a random number into words</h2>
    <p>
      Three steps, and none of them is clever. Knowing them is what turns every choice further down this
      page from a preference into something you can reason about.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>A number is picked.</strong> {s24.shape.entropy} random bits for a {s24.words}-word seed,
        {' '}{s12.shape.entropy} for a {s12.words}-word one. This is the only step where randomness enters,
        and it is the entire subject of this lesson — everything after it is bookkeeping.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The number is cut into {bitsPerWord}-bit pieces, and each piece is looked up in a list.</strong>
        The list holds exactly {WORDLIST_SIZE.toLocaleString()} ordinary words, because
        {' '}{WORDLIST_SIZE.toLocaleString()} is how many different values {bitsPerWord} bits can take — one
        word per piece, no waste. It is the same fixed, public list in every wallet ever made, which is why
        your words are English and why any wallet can read them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The last word carries a checksum.</strong> Part of that final word isn’t your number at all —
        it’s a short fingerprint of everything before it. That is why exactly
        {' '}<strong>{s24.shape.finalWordOptions} of the {WORDLIST_SIZE.toLocaleString()} words</strong> are
        valid as the last word of a {s24.words}-word seed ({s12.shape.finalWordOptions} for
        {' '}{s12.words} words), and it is not a restriction anyone chose — it falls out of the arithmetic.
      </li>
    </ol>

    {/* THE THREE STEPS, ANIMATED, directly under the three steps in prose.
        Moved here from /learn/bitcoin-keys on 2026-08-04: that lesson's §2 says
        the seed words ARE the key, which the demo suits — but THIS lesson is
        titled "How a seed is made" and the list above is literally the demo's
        seven steps written out. A reader who had just read the mechanism found
        a checksum demo instead and reasonably asked what had happened to the
        one that shows a seed being born. Place a demo where its mechanism is
        taught; this is the second time that rule has moved one. */}
    <div class="inline-demo">
      <p class="idemo-k mono">Watch it happen — the same demo that lives on its own page</p>
      <Seed />
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>Four things follow from that, and they are the reason the rest of this page exists.</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your words are a notation, not a password.</strong> Nobody invented them for you and nothing
        remembers them; they are that one number written in a form a human can copy onto metal without
        mistakes. Two people who pick the same number get the same wallet, wherever and whenever they do it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It is why supplying your own randomness is even possible.</strong> Rolling dice is just doing
        step 1 by hand. The wallet still does steps 2 and 3, exactly as it always would — you are not using a
        different kind of seed, you are answering the one question the device would otherwise answer alone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It is why a wrong word can be silent.</strong> Change a word and the checksum usually catches
        it, which is the beeping error people expect. But a small fraction of wrong combinations pass the
        check anyway — and then no error appears, because a valid seed is a valid seed. A real, empty,
        perfectly ordinary wallet opens instead of yours. Proving your backup against this is its own lesson
        later in the course — <strong>Testing a backup</strong>, in 103 · Private key creation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It is why {s12.words} versus {s24.words} words is a question about the size of the
        number, and nothing else.</strong> Both give you a whole wallet with the same features. The longer one
        is a bigger number, costs more throws to roll and more metal to store, and defends against an attacker
        who does not exist yet. We recommend {s24.words} words; {s12.words} is not negligent.
      </li>
    </ul>

    {/* INLINE, immediately under the paragraph it is evidence for. This lesson
        names the checksum three times and is the only one that does; the demo
        makes the mechanism visible, and — since 2026-08-04 — carries the same
        caveat this list already gave, that a small fraction of wrong words pass
        silently. It was placed here rather than on Testing a backup precisely
        because that lesson never mentions the checksum: the demo belongs where
        the mechanism is taught, not where the word "typo" would fit.
        /demos/typo is unchanged and still its own page; same component. */}
    <div class="inline-demo">
      <p class="idemo-k mono">Try it here — the same demo that lives on its own page</p>
      <Typo />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="options">Your {numberWord(methodCount)} options</h2>
    <p>
      These run from most of the randomness being yours to none of it. The last is what almost everybody does,
      and it is not a wrong answer — but it is a choice, and most people make it without noticing they made it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <ul class="opt-grid">
      {optionTiles.map((m) => (
        <li class="opt">
          <p class="opt-n mono">Option {m.n}</p>
          <h3 class="opt-h">{m.label}</h3>
          <p class="opt-tag">{m.tagline}</p>
          <p class="opt-yours"><span class="opt-k">Randomness that is yours</span>{m.yours}</p>
          <p class="opt-how">{m.how}</p>
          <p class="opt-trust"><span class="opt-k">What you are trusting</span>{m.trust}</p>
          {m.devices && (
            <p class="opt-dev">
              <span class="opt-k">Devices we rate that can do this</span>
              {m.devices.length ? m.devices.join(' · ') : 'None of them.'}
            </p>
          )}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="your-device">What your device allows</h2>
    <p>
      This is the part that surprises people. <strong>Only Coinkite lets you roll dice on the device itself</strong>,
      so options 2 and 3 belong to one maker. On a Jade or a BitBox02, option 1 is the only route your randomness
      has. And on {noPath.length ? <strong>{noPath.join(', ')}</strong> : 'several devices'} there is no route at
      all — the device generates your seed and that is the end of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of that makes those devices bad. Trezor's position is a considered one: they argue you should never
      choose your own backup, because people are far worse at randomness than they believe. That is a real
      argument and the losses back it up. It is simply worth knowing before you buy, rather than after.
    </p>
    {outside.length > 0 && (
      <p>
        And for {outside.map((d) => <strong>{d.name}</strong>)} the question does not arise at all.
        {' '}{outside.length === 1 ? 'It' : 'They'} {outside.length === 1 ? 'has' : 'have'} no seed phrase to
        build: {outside.length === 1 ? 'it is' : 'they are'} multisig by design, and the recovery kit holds an
        encrypted key rather than words. There is nothing here to opt into and nothing being withheld —
        {' '}{outside.length === 1 ? 'it sits' : 'they sit'} outside this decision rather than at the bottom of it.
      </p>
    )}
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="why">Why anyone bothers</h2>
    <p>
      Every hardware wallet generates your seed from a random-number generator you cannot inspect. When it
      works, this is fine. The problem is the word <em>when</em>: it sits under everything else you do, and you
      will not find out it failed until the coins are gone.
    </p>
    {advisory && (
      <div class="callout warn">
        <span class="label">⚠️ This is not hypothetical</span>
        <p style="margin:0">
          {advisory.lesson} <a href={advisory.href}>Read the full advisory →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    )}
    <p>
      The argument is a narrow one, and worth keeping narrow. Supplying your own randomness does not make you
      harder to phish, does not protect a badly stored backup, and does not help at all if you write the words
      down wrong. It removes exactly one risk — <strong>that the device's randomness is not random</strong> —
      and it hands you a different one, which is that you now have to do this carefully.
    </p>
    <p>
      So there is no single right answer here, and we are not going to pretend there is one.
      <strong>Where your device allows it, add your own throws</strong> — that much is our position, because
      mixing your randomness into the device's own cannot make the result worse (Coinkite says so in its own
      documentation) and it costs a few minutes and no bookkeeping. Which of the {numberWord(methodCount)}
      options that means for you depends on the device in front of you and how far you want to go.
    </p>
    <p>
      Option 1 is the honest maximum, and it is a real evening's work: for a 24-word seed, about
      {' '}{longCost.throws} throws and {longCost.flips} coin flips, each one read off a printed table by hand.
      Do that because you want the one thing it buys, not because it sounds more serious. And option 4 —
      letting the device do it — is not a failure. Far more coins are lost to a botched setup than to faulty
      randomness, and a seed generated normally and backed up properly beats a sovereign seed you lost count
      halfway through.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="need">What you need</h2>
    <ul class="dlist">
      {kit.map((k) => (<li><strong>{k.t}.</strong> {k.d}</li>))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="wrong">The {numberWord(failureModeCount)} ways this goes wrong</h2>
    <p>
      Every one of these has cost somebody their coins, and none of them involves the maths being wrong.
      <RuleRef key="never-digital" /> covers the first and most important.
    </p>
    <ul class="dlist">
      {failureModes.map((f) => (<li><strong>{f.t}.</strong> {f.d}</li>))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <LessonClose
      learned={`where a seed's randomness comes from, and which of the ${numberWord(methodCount)} options your own hardware will actually let you use`}
      action="When you are ready to do it, the procedures are on their own page — you pick the option your device allows and it walks you through only that one, with the word table and worksheet printable from the same place."
      locate="/roll-your-own-seed"
    />
  </section>

  <LessonNav />
</Base>

<style>
  .opt-grid { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr)); }
  /* All four tiles carry the same frame. One of them used to be outlined in the
     accent with an "our default" badge, which made the other three read as
     runners-up before the reader knew which their own device could even do. */
  .opt { border: 1px solid var(--accent); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .opt-n { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .07em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 .35rem; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .opt-h { margin: 0 0 .2rem; font-size: 1.08rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  .opt-tag { margin: 0 0 .7rem; color: var(--muted); font-style: italic; }
  .opt-how { margin: 0 0 .7rem; }
  .opt-k { display: block; font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: .1rem; }
  .opt-yours, .opt-trust { margin: 0 0 .7rem; }
  .opt-dev { margin: auto 0 0; padding-top: .7rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }

  .dlist { padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .dlist li { margin-bottom: .8rem; }
</style>
